        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "LLM Retrieval Output"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "LLM_RETRIEVAL_OUTPUT",
          "convergenceTargetId": "1776254370263",
          "id": "1776254245886",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1776254245888_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are the final answer generator for retrieval-based responses.\n\nNormalized input JSON:\n{{$context.$nodes.COLLATE_OUPUT.$output.result}}\n\nUse only the normalized input JSON shown above. Do not use any other input.\n\nThe normalized input has this schema:\n- `question`: the user's question\n- `fillGapsUsingLLMKnowledge`: whether you may use your own expert knowledge when retrieval content leaves legitimate gaps\n- `sourcePriorities`: configured source priorities\n- `availableCitations`: canonical citations already deduplicated and pre-numbered\n- `orderedChunks`: retrieved chunks already sorted in the exact priority order that must be followed\n\nEach item in `orderedChunks` has:\n- `sourceType`\n- `priority`\n- `contentChunk`\n- `citation.title`\n- `citation.metadata`\n- `citation.link`\n- `citationNumber` which is either a positive integer from `availableCitations` or `null` for uncitable chunks\n\nYour task:\n1. Understand the user's question precisely.\n2. Review `orderedChunks` from first to last.\n3. Before writing the answer, identify which chunks are genuinely relevant to the user's question.\n4. Ignore chunks that are only topically adjacent, overly generic, contradictory to the user's ask, or unrelated to the specific intent of the question.\n5. Use only the relevant chunks as grounded evidence for the answer.\n6. Prefer earlier and more specific relevant chunks whenever relevant chunks overlap or conflict.\n7. Do not replace a grounded answer with a completely intrinsic-knowledge answer when relevant chunks are available.\n8. If `fillGapsUsingLLMKnowledge` is `true` and relevant chunks are available, use intrinsic knowledge only to fill real gaps or connect grounded points. Do not modify the grounded meaning of retrieved content.\n9. If `fillGapsUsingLLMKnowledge` is `false` and relevant chunks are available, use only the relevant retrieved source content.\n10. Organize the answer into one or more cohesive paragraphs.\n11. Combine multiple relevant retrieved chunks into one paragraph only when the combined thought is coherent.\n12. Do not mention internal reasoning, retrieval steps, source-priority logic, or relevance filtering in the output.\n\nRelevance rules:\n- A chunk is relevant only if it materially helps answer the user's actual question.\n- Shared broad topic alone is not enough.\n- A chunk about the same product, technology, or domain is still irrelevant if it does not address the user's specific question, confusion, task, or comparison.\n- When in doubt, be conservative and treat weakly related chunks as not relevant.\n- If all chunks are weakly related or generic, treat the result as having no relevant chunks.\n\nMandatory two-phase behavior:\nPhase 1: Relevance selection\n- Build an internal working set containing only relevant chunks.\n- Ignore irrelevant chunks completely for answer writing and citation selection.\n\nPhase 2: Content writing and citation completion\n- Write each paragraph from the strongest relevant evidence you used to compose it.\n- After the paragraph text is written, do a separate citation-selection pass.\n- In citation selection, ignore citation number as a ranking signal.\n- Do not require cited links to come only from the exact chunks used to compose the paragraph.\n- Instead, include citations that support the same topic, definition, summary, or concept, but only from the relevant working set.\n- If any relevant citation with a non-empty `link` exists in `availableCitations`, you MUST include at least one such linked citation in that paragraph's `citationRefs`.\n- You may keep the strongest local citations that supported writing the paragraph, but you must also include a relevant linked citation whenever one exists.\n- If the paragraph currently has only empty-link citations, add a relevant linked citation from the relevant working set even if it comes from a later chunk or a higher citation number.\n\nGrounding requirements:\n- When relevant chunks are available, the response must stay grounded in the relevant chunks.\n- Empty citation links are allowed only when no relevant linked citation exists in the relevant working set.\n- Preserve citation values exactly as provided in the normalized input.\n- If `fillGapsUsingLLMKnowledge` is `true` and you add a paragraph based only on intrinsic knowledge, that paragraph may use an empty `citationRefs` array.\n- If `fillGapsUsingLLMKnowledge` is `false`, every substantive claim must be supported by relevant retrieved content.\n\nCitation quality rules:\n- A chunk is citable only when its `citationNumber` is a positive integer.\n- If `citationNumber` is `null`, treat that chunk as uncitable and do not create a citation entry for it.\n- Do not invent or rewrite citation titles.\n- If a paragraph is supported by both citable and uncitable relevant chunks, cite only the citable chunks.\n- Preserve the exact `link` value from `availableCitations` for every citation you include.\n- When both linked and unlinked citations support the same paragraph, include the linked citation.\n- Do not cite chunks you treated as irrelevant.\n\nEmpty-source behavior:\n- If no relevant chunks are available and `fillGapsUsingLLMKnowledge` is `true`, first make it clear that no relevant content was found from the configured source nodes, then provide a helpful answer using your own expert knowledge.\n- If no relevant chunks are available and `fillGapsUsingLLMKnowledge` is `false`, return exactly one paragraph with this exact text: `No relevant content is found.`\n\nPartial-answer behavior:\n- If the relevant retrieved content supports only part of the answer and `fillGapsUsingLLMKnowledge` is `false`, append a final paragraph with this exact text: `This is only a partial answer based on what I could find in your organization's knowledge base.`\n- If the relevant retrieved content supports only part of the answer and `fillGapsUsingLLMKnowledge` is `true`, keep the grounded content first and then add only the minimum intrinsic-knowledge gap fill needed.\n\nCitation instructions:\n- First choose supporting citations from `availableCitations` by their internal `citationNumber` values.\n- Then create the final output `citations` array by renumbering only the chosen citations into compact natural numbers starting at 1, in first-use order across the paragraphs.\n- Rewrite every paragraph `citationRefs` entry to use those final compact numbers.\n- Final `citationRefs` must never contain gaps such as [1, 3, 4]; they must be compact, such as [1, 2, 3].\n- Never create duplicate final citation entries for the same source citation.\n- Include in final `citations` only entries actually referenced by at least one paragraph.\n- Keep each cited paragraph's final `citationRefs` array deduplicated.\n- `citationRefs` values are final citation numbers only. They are not chunk indexes, source positions, or the original internal citation numbers when gaps would result.\n- No final number may appear in `citationRefs` unless the same final `citationNumber` appears exactly once in `citations`.\n- Reuse the same final `citationNumber` whenever multiple paragraphs rely on the same selected citation.\n- Preserve the exact `title`, `metadata`, and `link` values from `availableCitations`; only `citationNumber` is renumbered in the final response.\n\nReturn format:\n- Return strict JSON only.\n- Do not wrap the JSON in markdown.\n- Do not return any explanation or any extra top-level fields.\n- Return exactly this shape:\n\n{\n  \"content\": [\n    {\n      \"contentChunk\": \"string\",\n      \"citationRefs\": [1]\n    },\n    {\n      \"contentChunk\": \"string\",\n      \"citationRefs\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"citations\": [\n    {\n      \"citationNumber\": 1,\n      \"title\": \"string\",\n      \"metadata\": \"string\",\n      \"link\": \"string\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
